# The Weave

The **weave** takes the whole folder tree and produces ONE shippable thing:
`salesapp.html`. The command is `work bundle`, and it does three jobs in order —
**resolve** every reference, **audit** every capability, **run** every test — then
emits the bundle. A red test ships no bundle.

server compute :weave do
  def build(tree) do
    with {:ok, graph} <- resolve_refs(tree),   # every [[ref]] and :atom resolves
         {:ok, _caps} <- audit_caps(graph),    # every grant is fulfillable
         {:ok, units} <- compile_units(graph), # each language → wasm
         {:ok, :green} <- run_tests(units) do
      {:ok, emit_html(units, graph)}
    end
  end
end

## Polyglot, honored not flattened

The workbook is five languages, and the weave places each by its **placement**:

- **client islands** — [[Score chart]] (Svelte) and [[Trend sparkline]] (Solid)
  hydrate in the browser. Two frameworks, one document.
- **foreign server units** — [[Forecast]] (Rust+Zig) and [[Risk]] (Python) run as
  **sandboxed wasm** beside the BEAM.
- **server Elixir runs native** — [[Pipeline]], [[Score]], [[Enrich]] on the BEAM.

The weave never says "server runs as wasm": server Elixir is native, foreign server
units are wasm *beside* it, client units are wasm *in the browser*. One bundle, three
execution shapes, one authoring model.

Each polyglot unit gets its own file next — [[Score chart]], [[Trend sparkline]],
[[Forecast]], [[Risk]] — then [[Deploy]] ships the result.
